Node ID=900 friend joan standard
Text=Jehanne d'Arc died at the crossroads outside Barcelona, at a druid's standard. She offered to give more than it took, and you told her to do it. An English army woke in the same moment and did not know her name. The Crypt she had held for two hundred years went unguarded. Most of the men would not speak of that road. One of them paid for a mass at Rennes every year.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend golem standard
Text=The golem ended at the crossroads outside Barcelona, on your order. It turned its head and waited to be sure, and you were. It had been built in England to break a line. It broke the thing that was holding this one. The men who woke there never learned what freed them. A blue shoulder plate turned up in the paving. A church in Cornwall kept it as a relic, not knowing what it was.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 order none
Text=Every order came out of the siege smaller than it had gone in, and every one of them claimed the ending as its own. You had taken no oath and worn no mark. No faction anywhere could produce a document with your name on it. The Templars wrote that a knight had finished it. The Inquisition wrote that a faithful servant had. The Wielders wrote that one of their own had. The Horde told it as a story about a westerner who had owed them a favour. All four accounts survive in the archives, and not one of them is true.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 order england
Text=England spent that year waiting on Barcelona. In the end the Armada never had to sail into a city that was already burning. You had carried the Crown's business through the Port District while the fleet was still in harbour. Any week of it would have hanged you. Guy Fawkes' promise was kept the way such promises are kept. There was a pension paid out of an account with another man's name on it, and a quiet word in two ports. There was no record anywhere of the reason for either. He was hanged for something else, years later, and said nothing about you.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 order england duke
Text=England spent that year waiting on Barcelona. In the end the Armada never had to sail into a city that was already burning. You had carried the Crown's business through the Port District while the fleet was still in harbour. Any week of it would have hanged you. You lured the Duke of Medina to a keg of gunpowder behind DaVinci's workshop. The Armada lost its head weeks before it was due to sail. Guy Fawkes' promise was kept the way such promises are kept. There was a pension paid out of an account with another man's name on it, and a quiet word in two ports. There was no record anywhere of the reason for either. He was hanged for something else, years later, and said nothing about you.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 order spain
Text=Guy Fawkes told you his real name outside the Gate District, and you killed him for it. The Duke of Medina thanked you personally and went back to his ship. Spain filed it as one more plot against the Duke. England lost an agent it had never admitted to having. History has no record of Guy Fawkes after that year, and never had any reason to.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 order omalley
Text=Grace O'Malley kept her life and her true name. The Crown learned neither from you. You had been given more than her silence to hold, at a point where selling it would have cost you nothing and paid well. She was still taking prizes off the English coast twenty years after Barcelona, under the name she had chosen. Her crews told the Barcelona story the entire time, with you in it. They told it badly, and she never once corrected them.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cervantes
Text=Miguel de Cervantes went home with DaVinci's quill and without the beast. He could explain neither. Years later he finished his long book about a madman who believed himself a knight. It sold badly and was copied everywhere. Near the end he sent the madman to Barcelona to be beaten. He never wrote down what the quill was. He died in Madrid, in debt, and his grave was lost for four hundred years.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cervantes dead
Text=Cervantes was buried where he fell, with the quill still on him. Whoever went through his pockets could not read. A bookseller in Valladolid later claimed to own a manuscript about a madman who believed himself a knight. He could never produce it. The book was never finished. A Templar inventory of his effects lists four notebooks, a sword and one quill. It values the quill at nothing. Nobody else ever saw the beast.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cervantes dead alone
Text=Cervantes died in Barcelona with the quill still on him. His novel was never finished and the beast was never slain. The quill went into an evidence chest in the Chambers of the Inquisition. A clerk entered it as a quill, and it is presumably there yet. Nobody in Spain has heard of him. Don Quixote de la Mancha exists in no library anywhere. The gap has no shape, so nobody has ever noticed it.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cervantes alone
Text=You listened to him about the beast and left him to it, as everybody else had. He hunted it alone. The Knights Templar arrested him for attacking city property. They released him once they decided he was harmless. He published anyway. The book is about a madman who believed himself a knight. Its author had once offered you the post of squire. No reader has ever needed to know that to laugh at it.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 shade feather
Text=You told Don Quixote what he was and he believed you, which is the only argument anybody won in Barcelona that year. He asked whether there had been quests he would not now get to. He was told that there had been, and took it a great deal better than the man who made him would have. A single feather came down where he had been standing, went along the floor, and out under the door. Cervantes did not write about it and did not pick the quill up again for eleven years. The book, when it finally came, is about a man whom nobody had conjured.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 shade killed
Text=You killed Don Quixote in front of the man who had made him. Cervantes watched it without saying anything, and then talked about it for thirty years. There was no body, and they buried something anyway. The plot was never charged for, because the parish never established what was in it. He had asked only that you address him correctly. You had, every time, which he mentioned to Cervantes more than once. He said he was a slayer of dragons. By the end that was nearly true. Nobody in the Port District ever asked what was in the coffin.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 shade persists
Text=Don Quixote was still at it twenty years later, by which time he had outlasted most of the people who had found him funny. He kept to the Port District and challenged whatever he was given to challenge. He was fed by a tavern that had decided he was good for custom and billed the city for his stool twice. Nobody ever told him what he was. Cervantes never did either, which may have been cowardice and may have been the kindest thing either of them managed. He is in the book. He does not know.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes
Text=Cortes came out of the cave with a mechanical arm and a share of a treasure. He paid Shylocke in full in front of witnesses. He burned the note himself. The arm wanted a smith twice a year. He paid that too, and never asked DaVinci for a discount. His next expedition stayed in preparation for the rest of his life. He talked about the cave instead, and the river before it.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes dead
Text=Cortes died with DaVinci's arm still on him, and the arm was what came home. A Templar sergeant carried it back to Barcelona because it was obviously worth something. DaVinci took it apart on his bench and did not rebuild it. He kept the hand in a drawer and showed it to visitors for years. He told the story wrong every time. The debt was never paid. Only you now knew where the cave was.
Should Have Voiceover=1

Requirement=!None
Reply Text=
Go to node ID=
Custom Action=CTriggerRelayAction
{
Relay Name=Fixt order epilogue relay
}
Is Default Reply=1
------------------------------------------------------------
Node ID=900 friend cortes vindicated
Text=You heard both men out and found for the conquistador. Shylocke did not get the ship. Cortes never sailed again regardless. He had no crew, one arm and no credit anywhere in Barcelona. He kept the ship tied up in sight of the tavern, and paid harbour fees for nine years. He told the story of the hearing to everybody. In his telling you were a judge, not a stranger.
Should Have Voiceover=1

